1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:01,909
Meet Tom.

2
00:00:01,959 --> 00:00:04,567
Tom just got a chip that
turns his thoughts into text.

3
00:00:04,617 --> 00:00:07,098
Today is his first meeting with it.

4
00:00:07,598 --> 00:00:09,688
TOM (THINKING): This meeting
could have been an email.

5
00:00:10,588 --> 00:00:12,167
It was also his last meeting.

6
00:00:12,767 --> 00:00:16,752
By the end of this video, you will
know how close this chip really is.

7
00:00:16,802 --> 00:00:19,495
And there is one thought
Tom must never, ever think.

8
00:00:19,545 --> 00:00:21,079
We will get to it.

9
00:00:21,579 --> 00:00:22,799
This is Patent Future.

10
00:00:22,849 --> 00:00:24,053
File number two.

11
00:00:24,103 --> 00:00:25,341
The Mind Reader.

12
00:00:26,241 --> 00:00:27,131
Science fiction?

13
00:00:27,181 --> 00:00:29,160
For some people, it is already real.

14
00:00:29,210 --> 00:00:31,480
Casey Harrell lost his voice to ALS.

15
00:00:31,530 --> 00:00:36,318
In 2024, surgeons in California placed
four tiny sensors in his brain.

16
00:00:36,618 --> 00:00:40,169
Two hundred and fifty-six electrodes
listen while he tries to speak.

17
00:00:40,219 --> 00:00:44,792
A computer turns the signals into words,
and reads them out in his old voice.

18
00:00:45,092 --> 00:00:47,785
It gets about ninety-seven
out of a hundred words right.

19
00:00:48,285 --> 00:00:52,430
In January 2024, Neuralink put
its first chip into a human.

20
00:00:52,480 --> 00:00:57,115
Noland Arbaugh, paralyzed from the
shoulders down, used it to play chess.

21
00:00:57,165 --> 00:00:58,419
With his mind.

22
00:00:58,469 --> 00:01:01,753
By early 2026, twenty-one people had one.

23
00:01:02,553 --> 00:01:03,815
But here is the catch.

24
00:01:03,865 --> 00:01:05,563
These chips do not read thoughts.

25
00:01:05,613 --> 00:01:08,404
They read the brain trying to
move the mouth, or the hand.

26
00:01:08,704 --> 00:01:10,752
Real mind reading would
mean your inner voice.

27
00:01:10,802 --> 00:01:12,652
The words you only say in your head.

28
00:01:12,952 --> 00:01:16,070
In 2025, scientists at
Stanford tried exactly that.

29
00:01:16,120 --> 00:01:18,916
They decoded inner speech, in real time.

30
00:01:18,966 --> 00:01:21,172
Up to seventy-four percent right.

31
00:01:21,472 --> 00:01:24,863
And they found the chip could pick
up thoughts nobody asked it to.

32
00:01:24,913 --> 00:01:25,585
So they built a lock.

33
00:01:25,635 --> 00:01:26,998
More on that later.

34
00:01:27,798 --> 00:01:29,134
What about without surgery?

35
00:01:29,184 --> 00:01:33,742
In Texas, volunteers lay in a brain scanner
for sixteen hours, listening to stories.

36
00:01:34,042 --> 00:01:38,625
Then an AI guessed what they were
hearing, and even what they imagined.

37
00:01:38,675 --> 00:01:39,297
Not word for word.

38
00:01:39,347 --> 00:01:40,172
But the gist.

39
00:01:40,472 --> 00:01:41,456
The weak spot?

40
00:01:41,506 --> 00:01:43,509
It only worked on people who trained it.

41
00:01:43,559 --> 00:01:44,585
And anyone could beat it.

42
00:01:44,635 --> 00:01:46,417
Just think about animals.

43
00:01:47,217 --> 00:01:48,722
Tom's second day with the chip.

44
00:01:48,772 --> 00:01:49,925
A job interview.

45
00:01:49,975 --> 00:01:53,858
And the classic question: where do
you see yourself in five years?

46
00:01:54,458 --> 00:01:55,526
TOM (THINKING): In your chair.

47
00:01:56,326 --> 00:01:58,323
Some thoughts should stay thoughts.

48
00:01:59,123 --> 00:02:00,721
So who owns your brain data?

49
00:02:00,771 --> 00:02:05,031
In 2024, Colorado became the first
US state to protect it by law.

50
00:02:05,081 --> 00:02:06,421
California followed.

51
00:02:06,471 --> 00:02:09,479
Chile even wrote neurorights
into its constitution.

52
00:02:10,279 --> 00:02:11,058
Our verdict.

53
00:02:11,108 --> 00:02:15,415
Chips that give people their voice back:
already here, and getting better fast.

54
00:02:15,465 --> 00:02:18,890
A chip that reads your inner
voice, every day, for everyone?

55
00:02:18,940 --> 00:02:20,548
Maybe in twenty years.

56
00:02:20,598 --> 00:02:22,398
Reading thoughts you want to keep?

57
00:02:22,448 --> 00:02:23,426
We hope never.

58
00:02:23,476 --> 00:02:24,954
That is our own estimate.

59
00:02:25,454 --> 00:02:26,355
And if it works?

60
00:02:26,405 --> 00:02:27,650
Messages you just think.

61
00:02:27,700 --> 00:02:28,895
Silent phone calls.

62
00:02:28,945 --> 00:02:30,720
Meetings where nobody says a word.

63
00:02:30,770 --> 00:02:32,141
Tom would love that.

64
00:02:32,641 --> 00:02:35,687
Which brings us back to the one
thought Tom must never think.

65
00:02:35,737 --> 00:02:37,192
Remember the lock?

66
00:02:37,592 --> 00:02:41,605
The Stanford chip only listens
after a secret thought.

67
00:02:41,655 --> 00:02:42,564
A mental password.

68
00:02:42,614 --> 00:02:44,372
Theirs was: chitty chitty bang bang.

69
00:02:44,772 --> 00:02:46,261
Tom picked his own password.

70
00:02:46,311 --> 00:02:48,813
Something he will never think by accident.

71
00:02:49,413 --> 00:02:50,667
TOM (THINKING): I love Mondays.

72
00:02:51,567 --> 00:02:53,101
Patent status: pending.

73
00:02:53,151 --> 00:02:54,545
Would you get the chip?

74
00:02:54,595 --> 00:02:55,932
Tell us in the comments.

75
00:02:56,332 --> 00:02:58,866
Next time: a test that tells
you the day you will die.

76
00:02:58,916 --> 00:03:00,837
Subscribe, so you do not miss it.
